    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Wait, sorry—could you clarify the enzyme part? You compared the enzyme to a goblin, but I need the actual technical steps for the lesson plan. Specifically, how does the cell structure breakdown facilitate the contact between the alliinase and the sulfoxides? I need the precise mechanism of the sulfur-to-sulfoxide conversion without the metaphors.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Apologies for the confusion. Let's get straight to the technical details. When an onion is cut, the cell walls are disrupted, which leads to the release of cellular contents, including the enzyme alliinase and the sulfur-containing compounds known as sulfoxides. The cell structure breakdown is crucial because it exposes these compounds to the enzyme.\n\nAlliinase is an enzyme that catalyzes the conversion of sulfoxides to sulfenic acids. However, in the presence of oxygen, these sulfenic acids quickly rearrange into sulfenic acid derivatives, which then react with another molecule of the sulfoxide to form propanethial S-oxide, also known as syn-propanethial-oxide.\n\nThe reaction can be summarized as follows:\n\n1. Sulfoxides + Alliinase → Sulfenic acids\n2. Sulfenic acids + Oxygen → Sulfenic acid derivatives\n3. Sulfenic acid derivatives + Sulfoxides → Propanethial S-oxide\n\nPropanethial S-oxide is a volatile compound, which means it easily evaporates into the air. When it comes into contact with the moisture in your eyes, it forms sulfuric acid, which irritates the eyes, causing the",
